    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d434">

                <pb facs="0807" n="759" xml:id="pg759"/>

                <head>434. Telegram From the Department of State to the
                        Embassy in Tanganyika<anchor n="1" xml:id="d434-f1"
                    />
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1965-09-02T17:21:00-04:00">September 2, 1965, 5:21 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline></opener>
                <p>575. Ref: Dar’s 394 to Dept.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d434-f2"/> Leo’s 386 to
                        Dept.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d434-f3"/> Lisbon’s 88 to Dept.<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d434-f4"/> Notal. While regrouping of moderate nationalist elements
                    desirable, particularly to counter MPLA,
                    believe on balance we should not get involved internal nationalist matters,
                    especially personal differences, by acting as go-between. Also danger of leak.
                    Similarly do not wish intervene in strictly African matter by appearing endorse
                    Savimbi’s request with Kapwepwe.
                    Therefore if reply to Savimbi required, you may wish suggest he contact
                    Nigerians or other appropriate African Mission.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#bold">Rusk</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d434-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d434-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Central Files, POL
                    30–2 ANG. Confidential; Priority; Limdis. Drafted by Looram and Officer in Charge of Angola,
                    Mozambique, and Portuguese Guinea Affairs Lambert Heyniger; cleared by Deputy
                    Director of the Office of Central African Affairs William E. Schaufele, Director of the Office of Central African
                    Affairs L. Dean Brown, and Funseth; and
                    approved by Deputy Director for Eastern African Affairs Edward W. Mulcahy.
                    Repeated to Leopoldville, Lusaka, and Lisbon.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d434-f2" n="2" xml:id="d434-f2-content">
                <p>Telegram 394 from Dar-es-Salaam, September 2, reported that Jonas Savimbi,
                    ex-Foreign Minister of the GRAE, had asked for
                    Embassy assistance in transmitting messages to Holden Roberto and Kapwepwe that he was ready to discuss
                    reconciliation. (Ibid.)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d434-f3" n="3" xml:id="d434-f3-content">
                <p>Dated August 30. (Ibid., POL TUN–US)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d434-f4" n="4" xml:id="d434-f4-content">
                <p>Dated September 1. (Ibid., POL PORT–US)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
